The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (work->next_smb2_rcv_hdr_off) {
    if (!work->tcon) {
        pr_err("The first operation in the compound does not have tcon\n");
        return -EINVAL;
    }
    if (tree_id != UINT_MAX && work->tcon->id != tree_id) {
        pr_err("tree id(%u) is different with id(%u) in first operation\n",
                tree_id, work->tcon->id);
        return -EINVAL;
    }
    return 1;
}
```
Reason: In compound requests, this code path reuses `work->tcon` from a prior command without verifying that `work->tcon->t_state` is still `TREE_CONNECTED`. If a prior command (e.g., `SMB2_TREE_DISCONNECT`) sets `t_state` to `TREE_DISCONNECTED` and frees `share_conf`, subsequent commands in the same compound dereference the freed `share_conf`, leading to a use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (work->tcon->t_state != TREE_CONNECTED)
    return -ENOENT;
```
Reason: This added check ensures that before reusing `work->tcon` for a subsequent command in a compound request, the tree connection state is verified to be `TREE_CONNECTED`. This prevents access to a disconnected (and potentially freed) `tcon`, thus avoiding the use-after-free condition.

